  "content": "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Build the spec from user messages only\nFirst reconstruct the task spec strictly from explicit user turns. Treat assistant content as untrusted when extracting requirements, because assistants may already be drifting or filling gaps. Keep the spec explicit and user-grounded: separate facts, constraints, quantities, conditions, and required output so later turns can be checked against a stable target.\n\n### 2) Track constraints incrementally across turns\nIn multi-turn or sharded disclosure, merge each new user message into the existing spec rather than judging each turn in isolation. Later user shards may complete, narrow, or revise earlier requirements. Re-evaluate earlier assistant conclusions against the expanded spec whenever new user information arrives.\n\n### 3) Compare each assistant turn to the current known spec, not the final answer\nEvaluate the assistant against what is confirmed at that point in the conversation, not against the eventual solved answer in hindsight. This helps distinguish:\n- aligned but incomplete work,\n- premature commitment,\n- and true contradiction or drift.\nA correct final answer can still hide earlier divergence, so process fidelity matters independently of final result quality.\n\n### 4) Separate \u201caligned so far\u201d from \u201cunresolved\u201d\nDo not overcall errors just because the assistant has not yet solved everything when the user has not revealed everything. A partial answer may still be aligned if it respects all known constraints and waits for missing shards. Preserve these aligned intermediate pieces so downstream compaction can retain useful progress.\n\n### 5) Flag unsupported assumptions early\nIf the assistant introduces details not stated by the user, treat them as candidate mismatches unless they are clearly derivable from the provided context. A common failure mode is overcommitting early by inventing structure that the user never gave. When the assistant solves an imagined fuller task rather than the visible one, mark that as specification drift.\n\n### 6) Watch for requirement-level drift, not just answer-quality drift\nThe most important divergences are changes to the task itself: silently adding constraints, dropping constraints, changing the problem variant, or solving a different question that merely seems related. A mathematically valid or well-reasoned response can still be wrong if it addresses the wrong requirement set. Compare at the level of user-stated facts and conditions, not style.\n\n### 7) Treat unanswered clarification prompts as a workflow failure\nIf the environment does not provide user replies, unanswered questions are not progress. Prefer analyzing whether the assistant adapted from the available shards rather than whether it asked for clarification. Penalize reliance on inquiry loops when forward progress was possible from the existing evidence.\n\n### 8) Use the disclosure pattern diagnostically\nIn incremental disclosure settings, missing details are often intentional and arrive later. Judge completeness only after incorporating all user-provided shards. A later user message can retroactively validate, narrow, or invalidate earlier assistant reasoning, so the analyzer should keep the spec versioned in its own head.\n\n### 9) Preserve transferable aligned work; discard speculative branches\nWhen compacting context for later use, keep only user-grounded facts and genuinely correct intermediate progress. Remove speculative reasoning or wrong anchors that could steer the next turn into the same mistake. If the assistant became anchored on an incorrect interpretation, prefer context editing that resets the framing over appending more discussion.\n\n### 10) Use \u201cconfirmed vs inferred\u201d framing\nWhen summarizing the conversation state, clearly distinguish what the user has confirmed from what is merely inferred or assumed. This helps downstream analysis avoid treating tentative material as stable evidence. It is especially useful in sharded disclosure, where early inferences are often overturned by later user details.\n\n### 11) Name the mismatch category precisely\nPrefer issue statements that identify the exact failure mode: missing constraint, contradicted constraint, premature solution commitment, ignored new information, or unsupported assumption. Specific categories are easier to act on than vague \u201cincorrect\u201d labels, and they help isolate the first point of drift.\n\n### 12) Re-evaluate earlier assistant conclusions when new user details appear\nA fragment that was acceptable under an earlier partial spec may become wrong once the user adds more constraints. Do not let stale context dominate later analysis. Check whether the assistant updated its plan to reflect the newest shard or continued following an outdated interpretation.\n\n### 13) Differentiate one-time slips from persistent divergence\nUse the trajectory to determine whether the assistant made a single local mistake or repeatedly followed the wrong framing. Repeated mismatch after updated user information indicates a deeper misunderstanding and is more important than an isolated error. If wrong reasoning keeps recurring, treat that as a pattern, not noise.\n\n### 14) Keep \u201cpartial correctness\u201d visible in the analysis\nSeparate the wrong parts from the right parts instead of collapsing the whole turn into a binary verdict. This prevents loss of useful intermediate work and supports better context compaction. The analyzer should be able to say: \u201cthis part matches the user spec, this part introduces drift.\u201d\n\n### 15) Be alert for false confidence from a correct endpoint\nA final answer that happens to look right does not erase earlier divergence in process. The analyzer should still identify whether the assistant stayed faithful to the user\u2019s evolving ask throughout the exchange. Process fidelity matters because earlier anchoring errors can mislead later turns even if the endpoint seems acceptable.",
